# UI rules

## Structure (split the reference single-file into these)
- `Landing.tsx` — clean compiler-feel first screen, diet toggle, and a
  collapsible "how this tool works" disclosure. Keep it sparse.
- `DecompileLog.tsx` — the four-stage READ / TOKENIZE / MEASURE / BUILD
  animation, data-derived, tap-to-skip. Must reflect the real dataset numbers.
- `QuestionCard.tsx` — option cards; in "show working" mode each shows the
  surviving-count and bits badge.
- `WorkingPanel.tsx` — gain ranking vs ASK_COST, candidate pool, entropy trace.
- `Results.tsx` — order card (waiter-ready: product, cut, explicit spice line,
  sides), nearest alternative, working trace, compression stats.

## Conventions
- The entropy meter belongs to the IDENTIFY phase only. Once the product is
  resolved (0 bits / pool of 1), switch to a settings tracker — never show a
  full meter while still asking questions. Identify vs configure are distinct
  phases and the UI must say which one it's in.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` — animations already gate on it, keep that.
- Mobile-first: this is demoed from a phone. Test narrow viewports.
- Show, don't assert: when the app makes a claim ("product identified"), the
  next thing on screen must be consistent with it. Never let the UI say "done"
  and then ask another question.
- Keep the "show working" transparency layer — it's the point of the project,
  not debug chrome. It should read as intentional.
